  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Introduction\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU)** cache is a classic data structure that evicts the item that has not been accessed for the longest period when the cache reaches its capacity. Python's `collections.OrderedDict` provides a powerful foundation for implementing an LRU cache because it maintains insertion order and allows moving keys to the end, which aligns naturally with the \"most recently used\" semantics.\n\nThis tutorial walks through building a complete `LRUCache` class, explains the behavior of `get` and `put`, covers edge cases, provides tests, and concludes with complexity analysis and a worked example.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: The Core Class\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\nfrom typing import Any, Optional\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded Least Recently Used (LRU) cache with O(1) get and put operations.\n    \n    Attributes:\n        capacity: The maximum number of key-value pairs the cache can hold.\n        cache: An OrderedDict where keys are cache keys and values are cache values.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        if capacity <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be a positive integer.\")\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache: OrderedDict[Any, Any] = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key: Any) -> Optional[Any]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Get the value associated with the key.\n        \n        - If the key exists in the cache, move it to the end (mark as most recently used)\n          and return its value.\n        - If the key does not exist, return None.\n        \n        Time complexity: O(1)\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return None\n        \n        # Move the accessed key to the end of the OrderedDict\n        # This marks it as the most recently used item.\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n\n    def put(self, key: Any, value: Any) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Insert or update a key-value pair in the cache.\n        \n        - If the key already exists, update its value and move it to the end.\n        - If the key does not exist and the cache is at capacity, evict the\n          least recently used item (the first item in the OrderedDict).",
